toms  went away about 30 to 50 percent rested  and about another 18 to 60% took some  form of medication and interestingly  only 3% called 911 really important to  go to the ER so that you can have  imaging done to determine is it a TI a  is it a stroke to look at what your risk  factors are  to make a plan for prevention what kind  of testing you need and what kind of  medication potentially don't ignore it  please don't ignore it okay up next one  of the most pervasive health conditions  in America asthma may is asthma and  allergy awareness month according to the  CDC in 2015 over 18 million adults and 6  million children had asthma that equates  to almost 8% of the population tara and  asthma attack can be really scary what  what are some of the symptoms that  people should be aware of for anyone  who's ever had it it is one of the most  horrible things to feel like you can't  breathe and so a description that I read  that I really liked is imagine trying to  breathe through a straw that's plugged  up with cotton that's what's happening  to the airways they're becoming inflamed  constricted tightened mucus can fill  them and it's very difficult to breathe  so people can feel short of breath they  can cough they can hear wheezing and  they can have chest tightness or chest  pain the nice thing about asthma is that  although it is a chronic disease it is  controllable for most people by taking  medications by understanding your  triggers it can't be cured but  preparation can help what sort of  preparation preparation is really key  for this chronic disease and so what we  like patients to do is to develop a  personalized written action plan with  their doctor and this really highlights  several things it highlights their  particular triggers and how to avoid  them how and when to take their asthma  medications how to recognize their  symptoms and when their symptoms might  be getting worse and then importantly  what action to take when they do get  worse when to 